# Pi sandbox and permissions

Env-specific facts; decisions and their triggers live in the chezmoi repo's `docs/decisions.md`.
If behavior seems changed, verify against the source and update its template in the chezmoi repo.

## Planning and permissions

- New sessions start in plan mode. In the normal sandbox, source writes stay
  disabled; build/cache artifacts use designated writable roots. A reviewed unsandboxed plan action is not OS-enforced read-only.
- `/execute` or approval of `submit_plan` enables execution. Leaving plan
  mode keeps running work alive, read-only (a child still starting is
  refused); a workflow there admits only read-only agents.
  `/plan` (any mode), `/remove-dir` and a session change stop children
  and sandbox processes; `/execute` in execute is a no-op. An approval is
  single-use and voided by a mode change before its call runs. Shift+Tab
  toggles `/plan` and `/execute`.
- Hard denial, inherited role scope and mode restrictions run before approval;
  approval cannot widen them.
- Shell commands execute inside the SRT profile, as sandboxed commands do in Claude
  Code, and run without review; the profile is the boundary. Only remote-mutating
  commands it cannot judge (pushes, publishes, non-read `gh` calls, data-sending
  `curl`/`wget`, remote shells and copies; `workflow/policy.mjs` owns the list) and
  `git commit` (the user commits) go to the classifier.
  Workspace file edits run without review once execution is approved.
- Classifier errors/uncertainty prompt the root session; unattended requests deny.
  A pending dialog is announced in the footer: the call fails after ten minutes;
  the dialog stays until answered.
- Inside the profile the environment is the host's minus the workflow's own
  variables, secret-named exports (`KEY|SECRET|TOKEN|PASSW|CREDENTIAL|AUTH`) and
  pre-sandbox hooks (`BASH_ENV`, `ENV`, `SHELLOPTS`, `PS4`, `LD_*`, `DYLD_*`);
  toolchain variables such as `GOPATH` arrive as exported.
- `workspace_bash` with `dangerouslyDisableSandbox: true` (Claude Code's flag) runs
  on the host, environment minus the workflow's own variables, reviewed by the
  classifier in plan mode too; read-only roles are refused; the row reads
  `Ran … · unsandboxed`. The docker socket needs it: no `--config`/`DOCKER_HOST`
  workaround reaches it.
- A host off the allowlist goes in the call's `allowed_domains`, not the
  unsandboxed flag: reviewed with the command and opened for it alone.
- Read-oriented checks can still write artifacts: test runners, bundlers and config
  loaders may create caches, generated files or temporary modules beside source.
  Preserve inherited `TMPDIR`; use supported tool loaders and output-directory
  options to put artifacts in scratch. Where supported, Vite's
  `--configLoader runner` avoids the bundled config's `node_modules/.vite-temp`;
  `cacheDir` or `TMPDIR` alone does not relocate that bundle. Diagnose the tool's
  write path, not just the command's intent; do not widen policy or rewrite every
  command as a blanket workaround.
- `run_in_background: true` on `workspace_bash` holds the call 10 s: a command
  ending inside that is answered like a foreground call and never notifies.
  A longer one returns its task id; at exit the model hears the
  status and last twenty lines (with its next tool result, or as a new turn).
  `workspace_task` `{ id, action: "output" | "stop" }` reads or stops it, `{ action:
  "list" }` lists tasks. `/plan`, `/remove-dir`
  and exiting pi stop tasks; detached `setsid` daemons and containers outlive that.
  If a mode change is blocked on unverified cleanup, kill orphans and restart.
- `/add-dir <path>` adds an editable directory and loads its `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md`
  into the system prompt, as Claude Code's does: edits need approved execution, its
  `.git`, `.pi` and relative denies (`.env`) are protected. Home, its ancestors, denied
  paths, symlinks out and directories already in scope are refused with the reason.
  `/remove-dir` drops one; state is per session; its skills need a restart with
  `--skill <path>`. Reads were never workspace-confined; only the deny list applies.
- Playwright alone runs outside SRT so Chromium can launch, with only PATH, HOME
  and the broker's scratch TMPDIR; its disposable profile is not containment.
  Approvals, denials, role checks and cleanup still apply.

Sensitive paths come from the shared denylist. File tools resolve symlinks and
confine edits to the workspace and session scratch; git metadata and live-loaded code (`~/.pi/agent`,
the repository `node_modules`) are write-protected in every mode, the
repository source editable once execution is approved. A refusal names its reason
(`Writes are disabled in this scope`, `Path denied by managed policy`, `File edits
must remain inside the workspace`); report it rather than re-submit the plan. The
denylist does not cover unknown credential locations: add new ones to shared
policy before exposing them to a session.
